The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
ret = btrfs_reserve_extent(root, len, len, fs_info->sectorsize,
                           0, alloc_hint, &ins, 1, 1);
if (ret == -EAGAIN) {
    ...
}
```
Reason:
The `btrfs_reserve_extent()` function returns `-EAGAIN` when there are no active zones available for allocation. In the direct I/O path, this error is not handled properly, causing it to spill over to userland instead of waiting for ongoing I/O operations to complete.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (ret == -EAGAIN) {
    ASSERT(btrfs_is_zoned(fs_info));
    wait_on_bit_io(&inode->root->fs_info->flags, BTRFS_FS_NEED_ZONE_FINISH,
                   TASK_UNINTERRUPTIBLE);
    goto again;
}
```
Reason:
These statements implement the logic to wait for ongoing I/O operations to finish before retrying the allocation. By checking for the `-EAGAIN` error and using `wait_on_bit_io()`, the code ensures that it waits for the necessary conditions to be met before attempting to reserve the extent again, thus preventing the error from being passed to userland.

